    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, improving (N00.9)</content>. Recovering as expected. Euvolemic with blood pressure 124/78. Creatinine 1.05 mg/dL (eGFR 99), from a peak of 1.78; BUN 18 mg/dL; potassium 4.3 mmol/L. C3 74 mg/dL, still low but rising. Urine red cells 25 per high-power field and protein-to-creatinine ratio 420 mg/g, both falling. Nothing atypical, so no biopsy. The one-week creatinine and potassium were missed; judging by today&apos;s results no harm came of it. [S]<list><item>Stop furosemide today.</item><item>Keep sodium below 2 g a day; fluids as he wishes.</item><item>C3, creatinine, potassium, urinalysis, and urine protein-to-creatinine ratio at six weeks.</item><item>Future blood tests booked for the morning of each visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Group A streptococcal pharyngitis (J02.0)</content>. Ten-day course completed; throat normal. Resolved. [S]<list><item>Come in for any sore throat with fever and no cough.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Standardized office blood pressure</td><td>Every visit; treat if 140/90 or higher while nephritic, or with symptoms</td><td>Completed today</td><td>124/78 mmHg on furosemide; no antihypertensive needed</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Volume status: weight, edema, lungs, jugular venous pressure</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Weight 61.2 kg; no edema; jugular venous pressure not raised</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Creatinine with eGFR, BUN, and potassium</td><td>At diagnosis, about weekly while creatinine is raised or on a diuretic, then at six to eight weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Creatinine 1.05 mg/dL (eGFR 99), BUN 18 mg/dL, potassium 4.3 mmol/L</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Creatinine with eGFR, BUN, and potassium, one week after the consultation</td><td>About weekly while creatinine is raised or on a diuretic</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Protocol deviation: the check planned for about a week after the consultation, while creatinine was still rising on furosemide, was not drawn because he missed the laboratory appointment. Judging by today&apos;s results (creatinine falling to 1.05 mg/dL, potassium 4.3 mmol/L) no harm resulted. Corrective action: blood tests are now booked for the morning of each visit, and he was asked to call if he cannot attend</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine microscopy for red cells and casts, and urine protein-to-creatinine ratio</td><td>At diagnosis, at six weeks, at six months, and at a year, until hematuria and proteinuria clear</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Red cells 25 per high-power field, no casts; protein-to-creatinine ratio 420 mg/g</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Complement C3 (with C4 at diagnosis)</td><td>At diagnosis, at about two weeks, and at six to eight weeks until normal</td><td>Completed today</td><td>C3 74 mg/dL, low but rising from 42</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Serology for other glomerular diseases (ANA, ANCA, anti-GBM) and kidney biopsy</td><td>Only if atypical: C3 low beyond eight weeks, creatinine still rising, nephrotic-range proteinuria persisting, or systemic features</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>C3 rising and creatinine falling; the course is typical</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram with Doppler for clinical or subclinical carditis</td><td>When joint symptoms or fever follow a streptococcal infection</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal at the consultation</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Loop diuretic safety: potassium, creatinine, dizziness, and when to stop</td><td>Within a week of starting and at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Potassium 4.3 mmol/L; furosemide stopped today now he is euvolemic</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication reconciliation: NSAID avoidance and dose review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No NSAIDs taken</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antibiotic course for group A streptococcus: completion and allergy</td><td>At start and at completion</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Ten-day course of penicillin V completed four days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sodium below 2 g a day, fluid limit while edematous, and daily weights</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Salt kept low; fluid limit and daily weights no longer needed</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Nephrology consultation</td><td>Follow-up by message, in person if atypical</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Results shared with Dr. Okonkwo-Brandt, who agrees with stopping the diuretic</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Swelling gone and back to his usual weight of about 61 kg within two weeks. Outcome by 2026-09-29: 61.2 kg with no edema at two weeks [S]</item><item>Blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg with no blood pressure medicine needed by six weeks. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item><item>Creatinine back to normal by six weeks. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item><item>C3 back to normal within eight weeks. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item><item>Urine protein-to-creatinine ratio below 150 mg/g. In progress; target 2026-10-27 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: KDIGO 2021 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Glomerular Diseases (Kidney Int 2021;100(4S):S1-S276) (2021); Revision of the Jones Criteria for the Diagnosis of Acute Rheumatic Fever in the Era of Doppler Echocardiography: a scientific statement (Gewitz MH, et al. Circulation 2015;131(20):1806-18) (2015); Clinical Practice Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Group A Streptococcal Pharyngitis: 2012 Update (Shulman ST, et al. Clin Infect Dis 2012;55(10):e86-102) (2012); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, chronic kidney disease and hypertension monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in two weeks, as booked. Six-week kidney review in four weeks with blood and urine tests that morning. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-13: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

